{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anuraj","familyName":"Maurya","name":"Maurya, Anuraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Legal AI: Benchmarking Mamba and Transformers for Statutory Classification and Case Law Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T17:38:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:03:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T15:52:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-08T01:15:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Statutory corpora and judicial decisions are growing faster than legal professionals can read them, while individual judgments often exceed the context limits of standard encoder models. Transformer architectures dominate legal NLP benchmarks, but their quadratic attention complexity can require truncating or fragmenting documents that demand whole-document reasoning. Selective state-space models (SSMs), such as Mamba, offer linear-time sequence modeling and are a promising alternative for long legal documents, yet their performance on legal classification and retrieval remains underexplored.\n We present a preliminary benchmark comparing Mamba and SSD-Mamba with BERT, DeBERTa, and Longformer across four legal classification tasks (ECtHR, EUR-Lex, SCOTUS, and ILDC/ILC) and two case-retrieval tasks (ECtHR and ILDC), using a shared windowing and aggregation pipeline. The strongest SSM performs within approximately 1.3 percentage points of the strongest transformer across tasks and metrics. SSD-Mamba achieves the best results on most metrics for ECtHR classification, ILDC classification, and ECtHR retrieval, while processing approximately 3 times more tokens per second than DeBERTa and 4 times more than Longformer. DeBERTa remains strongest on SCOTUS and EUR-Lex F1.\n These results are preliminary because they do not include variance estimates across random seeds or statistical significance testing. Rather than presenting a definitive ranking, we use these findings to motivate further evaluation with repeated-seed experiments, statistical testing, and controls for model capacity and computational efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:25:27Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:25:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:03:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Bates","name":"Bates, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Glockner","name":"Glockner, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Preslav","familyName":"Nakov","name":"Nakov, Preslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iryna","familyName":"Gurevych","name":"Gurevych, Iryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConspirED: A Dataset for Cognitive Traits of Conspiracy Theories and Large Language Model Safety"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T06:39:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:19:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T10:54:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:41:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conspiracy theories erode public trust in science and institutions while resisting debunking by evolving and absorbing counter-evidence. As AI-generated misinformation becomes increasingly sophisticated, understanding the rhetorical patterns in conspiratorial content is important for developing interventions such as targeted prebunking and assessing AI vulnerabilities. We introduce CONSPIRED (CONSPIR Evaluation Dataset), which captures the cognitive traits of conspiratorial ideation in multi-sentence excerpts (80-120 words) from online conspiracy articles, annotated using the CONSPIR cognitive framework. CONSPIRED is the first dataset of conspiratorial content annotated for general cognitive traits. Using CONSPIRED, we (i) develop computational models that identify conspiratorial traits and the dominant trait in text excerpts, and (ii) evaluate LLM robustness to conspiratorial inputs. We find that LLMs are readily misaligned by conspiratorial framing, reproducing its rhetorical patterns even when successfully deflecting comparable fact-checked misinformation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at TACL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:32:55Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:32:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:17:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Til","familyName":"Spreuer","name":"Spreuer, Til","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Hoppe","name":"Hoppe, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael T.","familyName":"Schaub","name":"Schaub, Michael T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Faster Inference of Cell Complexes from Flows via Matrix Factorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T07:32:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:40:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.23919/eusipco63237.2025.11226659","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the following inference problem: Given a set of edge-flow signals observed on a graph, lift the graph to a cell complex, such that the observed edge-flow signals can be represented as a sparse combination of gradient and curl flows on the cell complex. Specifically, we aim to augment the observed graph by a set of 2-cells (polygons encircled by closed, non-intersecting paths), such that the eigenvectors of the Hodge Laplacian of the associated cell complex provide a sparse, interpretable representation of the observed edge flows on the graph. As it has been shown that the general problem is NP-hard in prior work, we here develop a novel matrix-factorization-based heuristic to solve the problem. Using computational experiments, we demonstrate that our new approach is significantly less computationally expensive than prior heuristics, while achieving only marginally worse performance in most settings. In fact, we find that for specifically noisy settings, our new approach outperforms the previous state of the art in both solution quality and computational speed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 5 figures, accepted at EUSIPCO 2025 in Palermo, evaluation code available at https://github.com/til-spreuer/mfci-workflow"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-01T01:25:30Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:25:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-15T02:57:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20549","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20549","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20549","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihai","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Weihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedGR$^2$: Breaking the Data Barrier for Medical Reasoning via Generative Reward Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T08:41:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:45:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-08T05:05:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:12:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The application of Vision-Language Models (VLMs) in medicine is critically hampered by the scarcity of high-quality, expert-annotated data. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) on existing datasets often leads to poor generalization on unseen modalities and tasks, while Reinforcement Learning (RL), a promising alternative, is stymied by the lack of reliable reward signals in this data-scarce domain. To break this impasse, we introduce Generative Reward Learning for Medical Reasoning (MedGR$^2$), a novel framework that creates a self-improving virtuous cycle. MedGR$^2$ co-develops a data generator and a reward model, enabling the automated, continuous creation of high-quality, multi-modal medical data that serves as both a superior training source for SFT and RL. Our experiments demonstrate that SFT with MedGR$^2$-produced data already surpasses baselines trained on large-scale, human-curated datasets. Crucially, when leveraging this data for RL via Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), our model achieves state-of-the-art cross-modality and cross-task generalization, significantly outperforming specialized RL-based methods. Furthermore, our compact model, empowered by MedGR$^2$, achieves performance competitive with foundation models possessing over 10 times more parameters. MedGR$^2$ presents a new paradigm for data-efficient learning in high-stakes domains, transforming the problem from data scarcity to data generation and unlocking the full potential of RL for building truly generalizable medical AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20549","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:34:56Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:34:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:35:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20916","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20916","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20916","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenglong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Kaiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangfan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JingBo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, JingBo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SageLM: A Multi-aspect and Explainable Large Language Model for Speech Judgement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T15:47:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:45:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-10T07:45:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:19:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech-to-Speech (S2S) Large Language Models (LLMs) are foundational to natural human-computer interaction, enabling end-to-end spoken dialogue systems. However, evaluating these models remains a fundamental challenge. We propose \\texttt{SageLM}, an end-to-end, multi-aspect, and explainable speech LLM for comprehensive S2S LLMs evaluation. First, unlike cascaded approaches that disregard acoustic features, SageLM jointly assesses both semantic and acoustic dimensions. Second, it leverages rationale-based supervision to enhance explainability and guide model learning, achieving superior alignment with evaluation outcomes compared to rule-based reinforcement learning methods. Third, we introduce \\textit{SpeechFeedback}, a synthetic preference dataset, and employ a two-stage training paradigm to mitigate the scarcity of speech preference data. Trained on both semantic and acoustic dimensions, SageLM achieves an 82.79\\% agreement rate with human evaluators, outperforming cascaded and SLM-based baselines by at least 7.42\\% and 26.20\\%, respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20916","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:44:20Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:44:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-12T03:26:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weitao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Weitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhoufutu","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zhoufutu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehui","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohui","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Xiaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SUMMA: A Multimodal Large Language Model for Advertisement Summarization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T09:19:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:26:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T09:22:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:36:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding multimodal video ads is crucial for improving query-ad matching and relevance ranking on short video platforms, enhancing advertising effectiveness and user experience. However, the effective utilization of multimodal information with high commercial value still largely constrained by reliance on highly compressed video embeddings-has long been inadequate. To address this, we propose SUMMA (the abbreviation of Summarizing MultiModal Ads), a multimodal model that automatically processes video ads into summaries highlighting the content of highest commercial value, thus improving their comprehension and ranking in Douyin search-advertising systems. SUMMA is developed via a two-stage training strategy-multimodal supervised fine-tuning followed by reinforcement learning with a mixed reward mechanism-on domain-specific data containing video frames and ASR/OCR transcripts, generating commercially valuable and explainable summaries. We integrate SUMMA-generated summaries into our production pipeline, directly enhancing the candidate retrieval and relevance ranking stages in real search-advertising systems. Both offline and online experiments show substantial improvements over baselines, with online results indicating a statistically significant 1.5% increase in advertising revenue. Our work establishes a novel paradigm for condensing multimodal information into representative texts, effectively aligning visual ad content with user query intent in retrieval and recommendation scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:35:44Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:35:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:15:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00230","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00230","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00230","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linus","familyName":"Stuhlmann","name":"Stuhlmann, Linus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Alexander","familyName":"Saxer","name":"Saxer, Michael Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating the Effectiveness of Transformer Layers in Wav2Vec 2.0, XLS-R, and Whisper for Speaker Identification Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T20:39:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:07:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-28T19:00:24Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; This was a conducted student project at our univerity, we don't think this fulfills the requirements for a publication on arxiv"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:17:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study evaluates the performance of three advanced speech encoder models, Wav2Vec 2.0, XLS-R, and Whisper, in speaker identification tasks. By fine-tuning these models and analyzing their layer-wise representations using SVCCA, k-means clustering, and t-SNE visualizations, we found that Wav2Vec 2.0 and XLS-R capture speaker-specific features effectively in their early layers, with fine-tuning improving stability and performance. Whisper showed better performance in deeper layers. Additionally, we determined the optimal number of transformer layers for each model when fine-tuned for speaker identification tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This was a conducted student project at our univerity, we don't think this fulfills the requirements for a publication on arxiv"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00230","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:27:33Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:27:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:16:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiwen","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Peiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aske","familyName":"Plaat","name":"Plaat, Aske","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niki","familyName":"van Stein","name":"van Stein, Niki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoComposer: LLM Multi-agent Collaborative Music Composition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T14:15:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:03:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing AI Music composition tools are limited in generation duration, musical quality, and controllability. We introduce CoComposer, a multi-agent system that consists of five collaborating agents, each with a task based on the traditional music composition workflow. Using the AudioBox-Aesthetics system, we experimentally evaluate CoComposer on four compositional criteria. We test with three LLMs (GPT-4o, DeepSeek-V3-0324, Gemini-2.5-Flash), and find (1) that CoComposer outperforms existing multi-agent LLM-based systems in music quality, and (2) compared to a single-agent system, in production complexity. Compared to non- LLM MusicLM, CoComposer has better interpretability and editability, although MusicLM still produces better music."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:25:14Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:25:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-03T03:25:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaked","familyName":"Zychlinski","name":"Zychlinski, Shaked","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Whole New World: Creating a Parallel-Poisoned Web Only AI-Agents Can See"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T08:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:02:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces a novel attack vector that leverages website cloaking techniques to compromise autonomous web-browsing agents powered by Large Language Models (LLMs). As these agents become more prevalent, their unique and often homogenous digital fingerprints - comprising browser attributes, automation framework signatures, and network characteristics - create a new, distinguishable class of web traffic. The attack exploits this fingerprintability. A malicious website can identify an incoming request as originating from an AI agent and dynamically serve a different, \"cloaked\" version of its content. While human users see a benign webpage, the agent is presented with a visually identical page embedded with hidden, malicious instructions, such as indirect prompt injections. This mechanism allows adversaries to hijack agent behavior, leading to data exfiltration, malware execution, or misinformation propagation, all while remaining completely invisible to human users and conventional security crawlers. This work formalizes the threat model, details the mechanics of agent fingerprinting and cloaking, and discusses the profound security implications for the future of agentic AI, highlighting the urgent need for robust defenses against this stealthy and scalable attack."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:25:03Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:25:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-03T03:25:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21793","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21793","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21793","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher C.","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Christopher C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoE-Health: A Mixture of Experts Framework for Robust Multimodal Healthcare Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T17:17:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:45:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Healthcare systems generate diverse multimodal data, including Electronic Health Records (EHR), clinical notes, and medical images. Effectively leveraging this data for clinical prediction is challenging, particularly as real-world samples often present with varied or incomplete modalities. Existing approaches typically require complete modality data or rely on manual selection strategies, limiting their applicability in real-world clinical settings where data availability varies across patients and institutions. To address these limitations, we propose MoE-Health, a novel Mixture of Experts framework designed for robust multimodal fusion in healthcare prediction. MoE-Health architecture is specifically developed to handle samples with differing modalities and improve performance on critical clinical tasks. By leveraging specialized expert networks and a dynamic gating mechanism, our approach dynamically selects and combines relevant experts based on available data modalities, enabling flexible adaptation to varying data availability scenarios. We evaluate MoE-Health on the MIMIC-IV dataset across three critical clinical prediction tasks: in-hospital mortality prediction, long length of stay, and hospital readmission prediction. Experimental results demonstrate that MoE-Health achieves superior performance compared to existing multimodal fusion methods while maintaining robustness across different modality availability patterns. The framework effectively integrates multimodal information, offering improved predictive performance and robustness in handling heterogeneous and incomplete healthcare data, making it particularly suitable for deployment in diverse healthcare environments with heterogeneous data availability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to The 16th ACM Conference on Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, and Health Informatics (ACM-BCB 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21793","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:36:49Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:36:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-01T01:36:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21007","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21007","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21007","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mateusz","familyName":"Jaszczuk","name":"Jaszczuk, Mateusz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadia","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Nadia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rapid Mismatch Estimation via Neural Network Informed Variational Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T17:09:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:49:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With robots increasingly operating in human-centric environments, ensuring soft and safe physical interactions, whether with humans, surroundings, or other machines, is essential. While compliant hardware can facilitate such interactions, this work focuses on impedance controllers that allow torque-controlled robots to safely and passively respond to contact while accurately executing tasks. From inverse dynamics to quadratic programming-based controllers, the effectiveness of these methods relies on accurate dynamics models of the robot and the object it manipulates. Any model mismatch results in task failures and unsafe behaviors. Thus, we introduce Rapid Mismatch Estimation (RME), an adaptive, controller-agnostic, probabilistic framework that estimates end-effector dynamics mismatches online, without relying on external force-torque sensors. From the robot's proprioceptive feedback, a Neural Network Model Mismatch Estimator generates a prior for a Variational Inference solver, which rapidly converges to the unknown parameters while quantifying uncertainty. With a real 7-DoF manipulator driven by a state-of-the-art passive impedance controller, RME adapts to sudden changes in mass and center of mass at the end-effector in $\\sim400$ ms, in static and dynamic settings. We demonstrate RME in a collaborative scenario where a human attaches an unknown basket to the robot's end-effector and dynamically adds/removes heavy items, showcasing fast and safe adaptation to changing dynamics during physical interaction without any external sensory system."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at 9th Annual Conference on Robot Learning. Project Website - https://mateusz-jaszczuk.github.io/rme/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21007","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:46:35Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:46:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:46:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20762","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20762","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20762","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fachri Najm Noer","familyName":"Kartiman","name":"Kartiman, Fachri Najm Noer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Rasim","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaya","familyName":"Wihardi","name":"Wihardi, Yaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nurul","familyName":"Hasanah","name":"Hasanah, Nurul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oskar","familyName":"Natan","name":"Natan, Oskar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bambang","familyName":"Wahono","name":"Wahono, Bambang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taufik Ibnu","familyName":"Salim","name":"Salim, Taufik Ibnu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SKGE-SWIN: End-To-End Autonomous Vehicle Waypoint Prediction and Navigation Using Skip Stage Swin Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T13:17:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:37:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Focusing on the development of an end-to-end autonomous vehicle model with pixel-to-pixel context awareness, this research proposes the SKGE-Swin architecture. This architecture utilizes the Swin Transformer with a skip-stage mechanism to broaden feature representation globally and at various network levels. This approach enables the model to extract information from distant pixels by leveraging the Swin Transformer's Shifted Window-based Multi-head Self-Attention (SW-MSA) mechanism and to retain critical information from the initial to the final stages of feature extraction, thereby enhancing its capability to comprehend complex patterns in the vehicle's surroundings. The model is evaluated on the CARLA platform using adversarial scenarios to simulate real-world conditions. Experimental results demonstrate that the SKGE-Swin architecture achieves a superior Driving Score compared to previous methods. Furthermore, an ablation study will be conducted to evaluate the contribution of each architectural component, including the influence of skip connections and the use of the Swin Transformer, in improving model performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"keywords-multitask learning, autonomous driving, end-to-end learning, skip connections, swin transformer, self-attention mechanism. 12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20762","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:40:16Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:40:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:40:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20500","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.20500","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.20500","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structure-aware Hypergraph Transformer for Diagnosis Prediction in Electronic Health Records"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T07:37:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:22:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electronic Health Records (EHR) systematically organize patient health data through standardized medical codes, serving as a comprehensive and invaluable source for predictive modeling. Graph neural networks (GNNs) have demonstrated effectiveness in modeling interactions between medical codes within EHR. However, existing GNN-based methods are inadequate due to: a) their reliance on pairwise relations fails to capture the inherent higher-order dependencies in clinical data, and b) the localized message-passing scheme limits representation power. To address these issues, this paper proposes a novel Structure-aware HyperGraph Transformer (SHGT) framework following three-fold ideas: a) employing a hypergraph structural encoder to capture higher-order interactions among medical codes, b) integrating the Transformer architecture to reason over the entire hypergraph, and c) designing a tailored loss function incorporating hypergraph reconstruction to preserve the hypergraph's original structure. Experiments on real-world EHR datasets demonstrate that the proposed SHGT outperforms existing state-of-the-art models on diagnosis prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20500","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:33:42Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:33:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-29T01:33:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":13,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=(doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00141%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20468%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20916%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20582%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00230%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21793%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20762%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20500%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21007%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21372%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00124%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00132%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.20549)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25"}}